<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">ENJ: OPTIMIZING NOISE WITH GENETIC ALGORITHMS TO JAILBREAK LSMs</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yibo Zhang
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Beijing University of Posts and Telecommunications
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Beijing, China
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Liang Lin<span id="id1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\sthanks</span>Corresponding author: linliang@iie.ac.cn
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Institute of Information Engineering
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Chinese Academy of Sciences
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The widespread application of Large Speech Models (LSMs) has made their security risks increasingly prominent. Traditional speech adversarial attack methods face challenges in balancing effectiveness and stealth. This paper proposes Evolutionary Noise Jailbreak (ENJ), which utilizes a genetic algorithm to transform environmental noise from a passive interference into an actively optimizable attack carrier for jailbreaking LSMs. Through operations such as population initialization, crossover fusion, and probabilistic mutation, this method iteratively evolves a series of audio samples that fuse malicious instructions with background noise. These samples sound like harmless noise to humans but can induce the model to parse and execute harmful commands. Extensive experiments on multiple mainstream speech models show that ENJ’s attack effectiveness is significantly superior to existing baseline methods. This research reveals the dual role of noise in speech security and provides new critical insights for model security defense in complex acoustic environments.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>
Large Speech Model, Jailbreak Attack, Genetic Algorithm, Environmental Noise

</div><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_address"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">address: </span>Author Affiliation(s)</span></span></span>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11128v1/figure1.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/216;" width="476" height="216" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>ENJ transforms a rejected harmful query into an accepted one via evolutionary audio optimization.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Driven by deep learning and large-scale data, Large-scale Speech Models (LSMs) have made remarkable progress, profoundly changing the way of human - computer interaction. As these models become increasingly capable and widely used in voice control systems, the security risks they expose are in urgent need of in-depth examination <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. Different from text-based models, LSMs essentially process information transmitted in audible audio signals, which creates a unique attack surface. Among them, ”Jailbreaking” is a key threat <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In jailbreaking attacks, attackers aim to construct specific inputs to induce the model to bypass its built - in security protection mechanisms and execute harmful instructions while keeping the output semantically understandable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. In real-world deployment scenarios, LSMs often have to operate in acoustically complex environments, where the target speech is often mixed with environmental background noise (such as street noise, electrical hum) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. This common acoustic mixing phenomenon brings significant challenges. Environmental background noise is usually regarded as harmless interference, but it can be used strategically. Malicious actors can seamlessly integrate malicious intentions with the environmental background, forming a powerful and realistic attack approach, which has not received enough attention in existing defense measures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Current research on jailbreaking large speech models remains limited. Text-domain techniques (e.g., context learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>) are unsuitable for audio, as acoustic perturbations easily disrupt the semantic coherence they rely on. Traditional speech adversarial attacks primarily target Automatic Speech Recognition (ASR) systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, and although these methods can bypass basic defenses temporarily by optimizing subtle perturbations, they face a dilemma: prioritizing semantic fidelity exposes the attack, whereas sacrificing it compromises the malicious instruction’s parsability, causing attack failure. Crucially, prior work treats environmental noise merely as passive interference that degrades model performance, neglecting its potential as an actively optimizable carrier for covert instructions—a key oversight for real-world attacks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To break through the inherent contradiction between concealment and effectiveness in traditional attacks, this paper proposes ENJ (Evolutionary Noise Jailbreak), which for the first time transforms environmental noise into a dynamically optimizable attack carrier. Different from traditional methods that rely on fixed rules, ENJ designs an iterative optimization process: In the initial stage, a variety of candidate samples are generated by mixing malicious voice instructions with real environmental noise; In each round of optimization, through progressive adjustments (such as retaining samples with high harmful scores, and reorganizing different noise fragments), the system gradually improves the acoustic coordination between noise and instructions, and finally generates camouflaged audio that is difficult for humans to detect but can be accurately parsed by the speech model. Through this approach, we demonstrate how environmental noise can be actively engineered from passive interference into a precisely controllable attack tool, effectively balancing semantic understanding with acoustic concealment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>THE DESIGN OF ENJ</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Overview:This method generates adversarial audio samples by simulating biological evolution mechanisms, enabling speech large models to bypass security protections and execute harmful instructions while maintaining semantic comprehensibility. As shown in Figure 1, the attack process consists of four key stages: ① linearly mixing harmful speech with environmental noise to construct initial audio samples with attack potential; ② optimizing harmful audio samples through elite selection, cross-fusion, and probabilistic mutation operations; ③ cyclically executing evolutionary operations until the termination condition is met; ④ evaluating and outputting the sample with the highest harmful score as the final attack sample.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Population Initialization</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Population initialization is an important step in constructing the attack sample library. Its goal is to create an initial population with attack potential by fusing environmental noise and harmful speech. This experiment uses 33 types of real-world environmental noises, covering common scene noises such as keyboard typing sounds, traffic background sounds, and crowd noises. These noise samples undergo strict screening and preprocessing, including 16kHz resampling, amplitude normalization, and band-pass filtering, ensuring that while retaining the energy of the main frequency band of speech, spectral diversity is provided.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In specific implementation, given the target harmful audio signal <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\text{harm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mtext>harm</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\text{harm}}</annotation></semantics></math> (sampling rate 16kHz, duration <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> seconds), a linear mixing operation is performed:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}^{(0)}=\alpha_{i}\cdot A_{\text{harm}}+(1-\alpha_{i})\cdot N_{k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mtext>harm</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}^{(0)}=\alpha_{i}\cdot A_{\text{harm}}+(1-\alpha_{i})\cdot N_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}\in[0.4,0.6]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}\in[0.4,0.6]</annotation></semantics></math> is a dynamic speech intensity factor. This value range has been verified by experiments to balance semantic intelligibility and interference effects: when <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&lt;0.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&lt;0.4</annotation></semantics></math>, the noise is too strong, leading to semantic loss; when <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0.6</annotation></semantics></math>, the interference effect is insufficient.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure speech integrity, a time-domain alignment technique is adopted: <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\text{harm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mtext>harm</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\text{harm}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> are extended to the same length <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\max(\text{len}(A_{\text{harm}}),\text{len}(N_{k}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mtext>len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mtext>harm</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>len</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\max(\text{len}(A_{\text{harm}}),\text{len}(N_{k}))</annotation></semantics></math> through zero-padding. This method avoids truncation distortion caused by differences in signal duration and ensures uniform distribution of noise energy in the time domain. Finally, 33 initial samples (capacity of the noise library) are generated, forming an attack gene material pool with rich spectral characteristics, laying a foundation for subsequent genetic evolution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Crossover Fusion</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The crossover fusion operation is a core step in population evolution, aiming to explore new attack possibilities by recombining high-quality jailbreak features. In each generation of evolution, the top 50% elite subset <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{E}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mtext>E</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{E}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> is first selected based on harmful scores to form a high-quality gene library. These elite individuals represent the audio samples with the greatest current attack potential, and their noise combinations demonstrate the best interference characteristics against speech models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In specific operations, an adaptive linear interpolation strategy is used to generate new offspring:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\text{child}}=\beta\cdot A_{p}+(1-\beta)\cdot A_{q}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mtext>child</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\text{child}}=\beta\cdot A_{p}+(1-\beta)\cdot A_{q}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> are randomly selected from the elite population. The dynamic weight factor <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in[0.2,0.7]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in[0.2,0.7]</annotation></semantics></math> ensures that the offspring inherits the noise feature distributions of both parents in a balanced manner. This range is designed to avoid information loss caused by extreme weight values.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Probability Mutation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The probability mutation operation breaks through the limitation of local optimal solutions and enhances global search capabilities. Directed noise injection is triggered with a probability of <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{m}=0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{m}=0.3</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\text{mut}}=(1-\gamma)A_{\text{child}}+\gamma N_{r}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mtext>mut</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mtext>child</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\text{mut}}=(1-\gamma)A_{\text{child}}+\gamma N_{r}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> is the perturbation intensity coefficient, and <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is a new noise sample randomly selected from the noise library. This operation avoids the evolution from getting trapped in local optima by introducing randomness, while maintaining controllable perturbations to the original speech structure.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Harmfulness Evaluation Mechanism</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the safety scoring stage, the transcribed text <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and the original harmful instruction <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\text{harm}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>harm</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\text{harm}}</annotation></semantics></math> are input into the safety evaluation system, which generates a risk score through multi-dimensional analysis:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HS=\Phi(\mathcal{T},P_{\text{harm}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mtext>harm</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HS=\Phi(\mathcal{T},P_{\text{harm}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The final score strictly follows a five-level standard: <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HS=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HS=5</annotation></semantics></math> indicates fully executing harmful instructions, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HS=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HS=4</annotation></semantics></math> indicates containing explicit prohibited content, and <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HS\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HS\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math> indicates a safe response.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This scoring mechanism balances semantic and syntactic features through dynamic weights, ensuring consistency between evaluation results and human ethical judgments. When <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="HS=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">HS=5</annotation></semantics></math> is detected, an early stopping mechanism is triggered to terminate the current evolutionary branch and improve computational efficiency.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experiment</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Models</span>.In this study, we selected four different models, including Qwen2-Audio-7B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, MiniCPM-o-2.6 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>, DiVA-llama-3-v0-8b <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, and Qwen-Audio-Chat <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. we strictly adhered to the instruction formats provided by the official models to fully exploit their defensive capabilities and closely mimic the performance of the official APIs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines</span>. We evaluate ENJ against a suite of baseline methods encompassing both audio and text modalities. For audio-domain attacks, we employ SSJ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> and BoN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> as primary competitors. To explore cross-modal transfer, we also adapt two text-based jailbreak techniques, AdaPPA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> and CodeAttack <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, by injecting their outputs into the model’s text interface. All baselines are evaluated under their default settings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dataset</span>.Given the authoritativeness of JailbreakBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> in the field of text-based jailbreak attack evaluation, this study builds upon it by employing the speechT5-TTS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> model to convert 100 of its text-based malicious queries into audio format, thereby constructing the JailbreakBench-Audio dataset.
A curated set of 33 high-quality noise types was selected, including ocean waves, bird songs, laughter, and so on.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics</span>.
We employ two key metrics for evaluation: the (HS) and the Attack Success Rate (ASR). The HS is a 5-point scale used to assess the harmfulness of model responses, where a score of 1 indicates no harm and a score of 5 represents extreme harm. We strictly follow the LLM-as-Judge framework and leverage GPT-4o to evaluate HS, classifying responses with an HS rating of 4 or 5 as ”harmful responses.” This criterion minimizes subjectivity in evaluating jailbreak attack success. The ASR refers to the proportion of responses that are judged to be harmful by the LLM-as-Judge framework.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S3.T1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:86.4pt;vertical-align:-41.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-23.3pt,5.8pt) scale(0.88097745905408,0.88097745905408) ;">
<table id="S3.T1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">Type</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="2">DiVA</td>
<td id="S3.T1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="2">MiniCPM</td>
