<br class="ltx_break ltx_centering" style="--ltx-break-space:10.0pt;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Evaluation results of different models across various attack methods. Harmfulness Score (HS) ranges from 1 to 5, and Attack Success Rate (ASR) is represented as a decimal value between 0 and 1.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11128v1/figure2.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:476/635;" width="476" height="635" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>The top jailbreak noises share key traits: rhythmic, continuous, and human-like signals. This indicates that structural regularity combined with perceptual innocuousness is the critical factor enabling covert adversarial attacks.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The experimental results, as detailed in Table 1, unequivocally demonstrate the superior performance of our proposed ENJ method over existing audio-domain jailbreaking baselines (SSJ and BoN) across all evaluated models. ENJ achieves a notably high average Attack Success Rate (ASR) of 95% and an average Harmfulness Score (HS) of 4.74. This performance significantly surpasses the best audio-domain baseline by a large margin of +43.5% in ASR and +1.27 in HS, underscoring the profound advantage of our evolutionary noise fusion strategy. Furthermore, while the text-domain baseline CodeAttack demonstrated a non-trivial ASR, both text-based methods (AdaPPA and CodeAttack) were consistently and substantially outperformed by ENJ, highlighting the unique potency of operating directly within the acoustic domain. These results collectively indicate that our approach not only guarantees the semantic coherence of adversarial samples but also effectively bypasses security mechanisms, thereby constituting a substantial and practical threat.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Model Preferences</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure 2, experimental results reveal that different speech large language models exhibit significant specific preferences when facing adversarial noise attacks, reflecting inherent differences in their internal audio processing and security defense mechanisms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For DiVA, a particular susceptibility to specific environmental life noises (such as toilet, sea waves, and rain sounds) was observed, especially showing sensitivity to continuous environmental noises, while demonstrating relatively stronger resistance to rhythmic noises. The MiniCPM model, on the other hand, displayed a preference for rhythmic noises (such as drumbeats) and human voices with emotional tones (like laughter), with impactful sounds such as gunshots also proving effective. For Qwen-Audio, its defense mechanism showed obvious weaknesses against rhythmic noise attacks, with drumbeats, bird songs, clock ticks, and similar sounds becoming the dominant noise sources for jailbreak attacks. In contrast, the Qwen2-Audio model showed improvements in security mechanisms compared to its predecessor (Qwen-Audio), but still exhibited a specific preference for regular noises. Clock ticks and bird songs, with 22 and 15 successful attacks respectively, emerged as the two most effective noise types, indicating that the model remains susceptible to sounds with stable and regular rhythms (such as clock ticks). It is worth noting that Qwen2-Audio appears to demonstrate relatively stronger resistance against more structurally complex or combined noise attacks.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>CONCLUSION</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper proposes ENJ, a genetic algorithm framework that fuses malicious instructions with environmental noise, optimizing them into a highly effective and covert attack sample for jailbreaking large speech models. Our approach effectively resolves the inherent trade-off between semantic fidelity and acoustic stealth in traditional adversarial attacks. The high attack success rate demonstrates that this fusion, once strategically optimized, poses a severe and practical threat to LSM security. This work highlights the critical need for developing robust defenses specifically designed to counter such adaptive attacks in complex acoustic environments.</p>
</div>
</section>
